    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>Search for the <strong>best care home audit software</strong> and you will find lists that all crown a single winner. That is the wrong shape for the question, because care homes want different things. This is an honest roundup: the real categories, what each is genuinely good for, and where quickler fits. Where we could not verify a vendor's exact feature set, we describe it in general terms and tell you to check their current offering.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>There is no single best care home audit software. The right tool depends on whether you need governance, template flexibility, or lightweight capture.</li><li>Dedicated care compliance platforms such as Radar Healthcare and Access Care Compliance are care governance and compliance platforms. They typically do more than audits alone. Check their current offering for exact features and pricing.</li><li>General inspection apps such as iAuditor and SafetyCulture are template-rich and capable, but they are apps with a login.</li><li>Paper and spreadsheets are free and familiar, but illegible, unsearchable and impossible to produce on demand.</li><li>Quickler is the no-app capture layer: audits run in WhatsApp, nothing to install, no login. It is <strong>not</strong> a full care governance platform, an eMAR or a care records system.</li><li>Quickler pricing is [[price:bundles]].</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Category one","heading":"Dedicated care compliance platforms","html":"<p>Platforms such as <strong>Radar Healthcare</strong> and <strong>Access Care Compliance</strong> are care governance and compliance platforms built for the sector. In general terms they are aimed at governance, quality, risk and incident management across a provider, and they typically cover far more than audits alone.</p><p>We could not verify their exact current feature sets or pricing, so we will not put specifics on them here. If your group needs a full care governance suite as a system of record, this is the right category to look at, and these platforms do more than quickler in that space. Check their current offering directly before deciding, and match it against what your governance team actually needs.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Category two","heading":"General inspection apps","html":"<p><strong>iAuditor</strong> and its parent <strong>SafetyCulture</strong> are the best known general inspection apps. They are template-rich, with flexible builders, clean reporting and strong dashboards, and they are used well beyond care. If you want a large library of templates and sophisticated form logic, this category is the stronger builder.</p><p>The tradeoff is the category itself. They are apps with a login. Every staff member installs the app and keeps a login alive, and on a care floor that is one more thing to open when hands are full, which is where on-site completion tends to slip. See our <a href=\"/pages/iauditor-alternative-uk.html\">iAuditor alternative</a> and <a href=\"/pages/safetyculture-alternative-uk.html\">SafetyCulture alternative</a> for the fuller teardown.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Category three","heading":"Paper and spreadsheets","html":"<p>Paper folders and spreadsheets are still common in UK care homes. They are free, familiar and need no training. The cost is not the subscription. It is that the records are illegible, unsearchable and impossible to produce quickly when an inspector or a regional manager asks. When the folder cannot be found, the audit effectively did not happen. For a group, paper can never give a single view across homes.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Category four","heading":"Quickler, the no-app capture layer","html":"<p><strong>Quickler</strong> removes the app. The audit arrives in the staff member's existing WhatsApp and they reply by text, voice note or photo. Nothing to install, no login. Voice notes are transcribed automatically, a photo can be attached against any finding as timestamped evidence, and each finished audit produces a shareable web report and a permanent record, with the group seeing every home on one dashboard.</p><p>Being fair about where it stops: quickler is not a drag-and-drop form builder with thousands of templates, and it is <strong>not</strong> a full care governance platform, an eMAR or a care records system. If your group needs a governance system of record, the dedicated care compliance platforms are the right category and do more than quickler. Quickler is the lightweight capture layer, and many homes run it alongside a governance platform. See <a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">care home audit software</a> and <a href=\"/pages/quickler-for-care-homes.html\">quickler for care homes</a>.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"How to choose","heading":"Match the tool to what is missing","html":"<p>Ask what is actually missing today. If it is a governance system of record across the group, look at a dedicated care compliance platform. If it is template flexibility and form logic, look at a general inspection app. If the audits get done but the evidence is thin because nobody opens an app on the floor, the missing piece is capture, and a no-app WhatsApp workflow is the thing to test.</p><p>These are not mutually exclusive. A common shape is a governance platform as the system of record, with a lightweight no-app capture layer feeding it clean, dated, photo-backed audits from the floor. Pricing for quickler is [[price:bundles]] An active user completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Best care home audit software, answered","html":"<h3>What is the best care home audit software in the UK?</h3><p>There is no single best, because care homes need different things. If you need a full care governance system of record, a dedicated care compliance platform such as Radar Healthcare or Access Care Compliance is the right category, and you should check their current offering. If you need template flexibility, a general inspection app such as iAuditor or SafetyCulture is the stronger builder. If you need lightweight capture with no app on the floor, quickler fills that gap.</p><h3>How does quickler compare to Radar Healthcare or Access Care Compliance?</h3><p>They are in different categories. Radar Healthcare and Access Care Compliance are care governance and compliance platforms that typically cover far more than audits alone, and they do more than quickler in governance and risk. We could not verify their exact features or pricing, so check their current offering. Quickler is the lightweight, no-app audit capture layer, and it can sit alongside a governance platform rather than replacing it.</p><h3>How does quickler compare to iAuditor or SafetyCulture?</h3><p>iAuditor and SafetyCulture are template-rich general inspection apps with a login. Quickler runs the audit in WhatsApp with no app and no login, which tends to hold up better for on-site completion, at the cost of less template flexibility. If your team will happily open an app, the inspection apps are a mature choice.</p><h3>Is quickler a care records or eMAR system?</h3><p>No. Quickler is an audit and evidence tool, not a care records, eMAR, clinical or full care governance system. It sits alongside those systems rather than replacing them.</p><h3>What does quickler cost?</h3><p>Pricing is £20 per active user per month with a £20 monthly minimum, plus a free tier of 20 reports a month, extra dormant users always free. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free. There is a free forever tier of 20 reports a month with up to 10 photos per report, no card and no trial clock.</p>"}
